Q: How to improve website loading speed using lazy loading in JavaScript? A: <p>You can fix this easily .....no heavy JavaScript needed.</p>
<p><strong>Best (simple solution): use native lazy loading</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;img src=&quot;image1.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;tempo traveller&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;image2.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;tempo traveller&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot;&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;placeholder.jpg&quot; data-src=&quot;image1.jpg&quot; class=&quot;lazy&quot; alt=&quot;tempo traveller&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>That’s it. Most modern browsers handle it automatically.</p>
<p><strong>If you want a JS fallback (for older browsers):</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;img data-src=&quot;image1.jpg&quot; class=&quot;lazy&quot; alt=&quot;tempo traveller&quot;&gt;
&lt;img data-src=&quot;image2.jpg&quot; class=&quot;lazy&quot; alt=&quot;tempo traveller&quot;&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
const imgs = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.lazy&quot;);

const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries =&gt; {
  entries.forEach(entry =&gt; {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
      const img = entry.target;
      img.src = img.dataset.src;
      observer.unobserve(img);
    }
  });
});

imgs.forEach(img =&gt; observer.observe(img));
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Combine this with compressed images (WebP) for best speed gains.</p>
